export const modernPerspective = [
  'Tight-binding now spans several distinct constructions: empirical parameter fits, symmetry-designed low-energy Hamiltonians, and Hamiltonians obtained by downfolding or Wannierization of first-principles calculations. Their matrices can look identical, but the meaning, gauge freedom, energy window, transferability, and validation burden are different.',
  'A small orthogonal basis is rarely both strictly short ranged and transferable. Orthogonalization generates longer-ranged tails, while compact nonorthogonal atomic-like functions often preserve locality more naturally. Truncation range, overlap treatment, and the chosen energy window therefore belong to the model definition rather than being harmless numerical details.',
  'Low-energy few-band models are strongest when the target question lives inside the subspace they retain. A model that reproduces a Dirac crossing or Fermi surface may still omit remote bands needed for optical matrix elements, screening, structural energetics, spin–orbit renormalization, or chemistry away from the fitted environment.',
