export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 14 replaces a delocalized representation by a matrix language tied to atoms and bonds. Translational symmetry turns real-space hopping and overlap matrices into finite k-dependent generalized eigenproblems. Orbital symmetry then compresses the enormous space of possible matrix elements into Slater–Koster channels, making geometry directly visible in the Hamiltonian. The resulting few-band models explain band widths, van Hove singularities, band crossings, graphene Dirac points, nanotube zone folding, and effective low-energy descriptions of more complicated compounds with remarkable economy. That economy has a price: fitted eigenvalues do not uniquely define a total-energy model, orthogonality changes locality, and parameters that work in one coordination environment need not transfer to another. Chapter 15 keeps the localized representation but removes the defining empirical shortcut by calculating the basis functions and matrix elements within full self-consistent electronic-structure methods.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-1-localized-orbitals">
  <h2 id="section-14-1-localized-orbitals">14.1 Localized Atom-Centered Orbitals</h2>
  <p>
    The plane-wave route describes a crystal with functions spread through the entire cell. A localized-orbital route starts from functions attached to particular atoms and asks how electrons move between them. The physical advantage is immediate: on-site terms describe atomic energy scales, off-site terms describe hybridization between neighboring sites, and the spatial decay of localized functions makes distant matrix elements small. This same language underlies both simple tight-binding models and the <a href={href('/theory/localized-orbital-methods/')}>full localized-basis calculations</a> developed in Chapter 15.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let orbitals labelled by <em>m</em> and <em>m′</em> sit in cells separated by a lattice translation. Their real-space Hamiltonian and overlap matrix elements are the basic data of the representation. Translational symmetry means that they depend on the separation of the cells rather than on an absolute cell label. The Hamiltonian element is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="localized Hamiltonian matrix element">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mo>∫</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo> </mo>
          <msubsup><mi>χ</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo>
          <mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover>
          <msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;mm'&#125;(\mathbf T)=\int\!\mathrm&#123;d&#125;\mathbf r\,\chi_m^*(\mathbf r-\tau_m)\hat H\chi_&#123;m'&#125;(\mathbf r-\tau_&#123;m'&#125;-\mathbf T)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The overlap matrix has the same structure with the Hamiltonian operator omitted. Unlike plane waves, atom-centered functions need not be orthogonal, so the overlap is part of the eigenproblem rather than an optional correction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Periodicity is restored by forming Bloch sums of each localized orbital. Their real-space matrix elements are Fourier transformed over lattice translations,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Fourier transform of localized matrices">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi></munder><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi></mrow></msup>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo>
          <mspace width="1em"/>
          <msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi></munder><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi></mrow></msup>
          <msub><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">T</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;mm'&#125;(\mathbf k)=\sum_\mathbf T \mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf T&#125;H_&#123;mm'&#125;(\mathbf T),\quad S_&#123;mm'&#125;(\mathbf k)=\sum_\mathbf T \mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf T&#125;S_&#123;mm'&#125;(\mathbf T)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each wavevector therefore remains an independent block, exactly as Bloch symmetry requires. The coefficients of the localized Bloch sums follow from the generalized eigenvalue problem
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="generalized tight binding eigenvalue equation">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow>
          <msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[H(\mathbf k)-\varepsilon_n(\mathbf k)S(\mathbf k)]c_n(\mathbf k)=0</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This equation is the central bridge between a localized real-space picture and the band structure of a periodic solid. Locality appears computationally as sparsity or rapid decay of the real-space matrices; periodicity appears as their phase-weighted Fourier transform.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-2-matrix-elements">
  <h2 id="section-14-2-matrix-elements">14.2 Matrix Elements with Atomic-Like Orbitals</h2>
  <p>
    Atomic-like orbitals make symmetry usable before any detailed radial integral is known. A function centered on an atom can be separated into a radial part and an angular function,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="atomic like orbital radial angular decomposition">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <msub><mi>χ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi_&#123;nlm&#125;(\mathbf r)=\chi_&#123;nl&#125;(r)Y_&#123;lm&#125;(\hat&#123;\mathbf r&#125;)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The angular momentum labels organize which matrix elements can exist. For a pair of atoms, choose the interatomic line as the local quantization axis. Cylindrical symmetry then classifies two-center matrix elements by the magnitude of angular momentum about that bond: σ for zero, π for one, δ for two, and so on. A large collection of Cartesian orbital couplings can therefore be generated from a much smaller set of irreducible radial integrals such as ssσ, spσ, ppσ, and ppπ.
  </p>
  <p>
    The directional dependence is pure geometry. For example, if the bond direction has direction cosine <em>l</em> along x, a p<sub>x</sub>–p<sub>x</sub> coupling is a mixture of σ and π channels,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Slater Koster pp x x matrix element">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo>
          <mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>π</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_&#123;p_xp_x&#125;=l^2K_&#123;pp\sigma&#125;+(1-l^2)K_&#123;pp\pi&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This separation between angular geometry and radial strength is the key simplification. True Hamiltonian matrix elements can also contain three-center and environment-dependent contributions because the potential comes from all atoms. Tight-binding models in this chapter deliberately replace that full integral problem by parameterized matrix elements constrained by symmetry; Chapter 15 returns to explicit basis functions and integrals.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-3-spin-orbit">
  <h2 id="section-14-3-spin-orbit">14.3 Spin–Orbit Interaction</h2>
  <p>
    Spin–orbit coupling provides an important example of how additional physics enters a localized Hamiltonian. In a spherically symmetric atomic potential, the leading one-electron term has the form
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="atomic spin orbit Hamiltonian">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>SO</mi></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac><msup><mi>ℏ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac>
          <mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac>
          <mi mathvariant="bold">L</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">σ</mi><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat H_&#123;SO&#125;=\frac&#123;\hbar^2&#125;&#123;4m^2c^2&#125;\frac1r\frac&#123;\mathrm&#123;d&#125;V&#125;&#123;\mathrm&#123;d&#125;r&#125;\mathbf L\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Because the radial derivative of the potential is largest near a nucleus, a localized basis with definite atomic angular character can represent the dominant effect by on-site spin-dependent matrices. The orbital basis is doubled in spin space and the eigenstates are generally spin mixtures. This is especially economical for p and d manifolds, but the fitted on-site spin–orbit parameter is a property of the solid-state model, not automatically the free-atom splitting.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction among scalar relativity, spin polarization, noncollinear spin, and spin–orbit coupling remains important. Adding an on-site spin–orbit matrix changes the Hamiltonian symmetry and can open or close band gaps; it does not by itself specify a magnetic ground state. The broader relativistic setting is linked in <a href={href('/theory/relativistic-electronic-structure-spin-and-magnetism/')}>Foundations</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-4-slater-koster">
  <h2 id="section-14-4-slater-koster">14.4 Slater–Koster Two-Center Approximation</h2>
  <p>
    Slater and Koster turned the symmetry analysis into a reusable model construction. The central approximation is to describe intersite Hamiltonian and overlap elements by two-center σ, π, δ, … functions of distance, while direction cosines provide the angular factors. Once the on-site levels and a small set of distance-dependent hopping parameters are specified, the Hamiltonian for many crystal structures can be assembled without evaluating explicit three-center integrals.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method is powerful because different scientific goals can use the same algebra with different parameter choices. Parameters fitted to eigenvalues at one lattice constant can interpolate a band structure very accurately. Parameters intended for structural energetics must instead vary with atomic positions and be constrained by energies, forces, elastic data, or phonons over a range of environments. These are different fitted objects. A band fit should not be assumed transferable to a distorted structure merely because both use the same Slater–Koster table.
  </p>
  <p>
    In this sense the two-center form is best viewed as a symmetry-aware coordinate system for matrix elements. Multicenter physics can be folded into fitted effective two-center terms at one configuration, but doing so generally makes those effective terms environment dependent. Section 14.12 returns to this transferability problem explicitly.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-5-single-s-band">
  <h2 id="section-14-5-single-s-band">14.5 Tight-Binding Bands: Example of a Single s Band</h2>
  <p>
    The single-orbital nearest-neighbor model strips the band problem down to geometry plus one hopping amplitude. For an orthogonal s orbital on every site of a simple cubic lattice, with nearest-neighbor hopping <em>t</em>, the dispersion is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="nearest neighbor simple cubic tight binding dispersion">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>ε</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi>
          <mrow><mo>[</mo><mo>cos</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>cos</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>cos</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\mathbf k)=2t[\cos(k_xa)+\cos(k_ya)+\cos(k_za)]</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Removing one or two cosine terms gives the line and square lattices. This immediately reveals how dimensionality changes bandwidth and density of states. In the square lattice, the band has saddle points at the zone-edge points and therefore a logarithmic van Hove singularity. At half filling, nearest-neighbor particle–hole symmetry places the Fermi level at the band center and produces a perfectly nested square Fermi surface. Longer-range hopping or overlap breaks these special symmetries.
  </p>
  <p>
    Nonorthogonality illustrates why overlap cannot be treated as a cosmetic detail. With nearest-neighbor overlap <em>s</em>, even this one-band problem becomes
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="nonorthogonal one band tight binding dispersion">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>ε</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mfrac>
            <mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow>
            <mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow>
          </mfrac><mo>,</mo>
          <mspace width="1em"/>
          <mi>C</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></munder><mo>cos</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon(\mathbf k)=\frac&#123;2tC(\mathbf k)&#125;&#123;1+2sC(\mathbf k)&#125;,\quad C(\mathbf k)=\sum_&#123;\alpha=x,y,z&#125;\cos(k_\alpha a)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The denominator destroys the simple energy-reflection symmetry and mimics some effects that an orthogonal model would need to represent using longer-ranged Hamiltonian elements. This anticipates the deeper locality–orthogonality trade-off discussed at the end of the chapter.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-6-two-band">
  <h2 id="section-14-6-two-band">14.6 Two-Band Models</h2>
  <p>
    Two-band Hamiltonians are the smallest matrices that can describe hybridization, avoided crossings, band inversion, and many boundary-state mechanisms. Their generic Hermitian form is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="generic two band Hamiltonian">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>H</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo>
          <mfenced open="(" close=")"><mtable>
            <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>t</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd></mtr>
            <mtr><mtd><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>*</mo></msup><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd></mtr>
          </mtable></mfenced><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbf k)=\begin&#123;pmatrix&#125;E_1(\mathbf k)&amp;t(\mathbf k)\\t^*(\mathbf k)&amp;E_2(\mathbf k)\end&#123;pmatrix&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The same matrix can represent two inequivalent sites, two orbitals of different parity on one site, bonding and antibonding combinations, or an effective low-energy subspace obtained after eliminating remote states. What matters physically is how symmetry constrains the diagonal difference and off-diagonal coupling. A crossing survives when symmetry forces the relevant coupling to vanish; otherwise hybridization produces level repulsion.
  </p>
  <p>
    Martin emphasizes these models because their algebra reappears in later chapters on surface states and topology. Their usefulness does not depend on topology: they are a compact language for any problem in which two states dominate the energy window of interest.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-7-graphene">
  <h2 id="section-14-7-graphene">14.7 Graphene</h2>
  <p>
    Graphene is the canonical example of a minimal tight-binding model giving both qualitative and semi-quantitative insight. Reflection symmetry separates the out-of-plane p<sub>z</sub>-derived π manifold from the in-plane σ states in the flat, spin–orbit-free limit. Because the honeycomb lattice has two equivalent carbon sites per primitive cell, the π problem is a two-sublattice Hamiltonian with zero on-site energy and nearest-neighbor hopping.
  </p>
  <p>
    For equivalent sublattices the two eigenvalues are simply
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="graphene nearest neighbor band energies">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>ε</mi><mo>±</mo></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mo>|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_\pm(\mathbf k)=\pm|t(\mathbf k)|</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    At the K and K′ corners of the Brillouin zone, the phase contributions from the three nearest-neighbor bonds cancel and the off-diagonal matrix element vanishes. The two bands therefore touch. Expanding around either touching point gives a linear dispersion, which motivates the Dirac-particle analogy. The analogy is structural rather than relativistic: the velocity is a band parameter and the spectrum becomes nonlinear away from the valley.
  </p>
  <p>
    A one-orbital π model cannot produce atomic spin–orbit coupling by itself because the retained state has no complete local p manifold. The physical spin–orbit effect appears after the other p-derived states are allowed to mix, so the small graphene gap is a higher-order consequence of degrees of freedom that the minimal model integrated out. This is a useful warning for every effective Hamiltonian: omitted states can renormalize the retained subspace even when they lie far from the target energy window.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-8-nanotubes">
  <h2 id="section-14-8-nanotubes">14.8 Nanotubes</h2>
  <p>
    A carbon nanotube turns the two-dimensional graphene band structure into a family of one-dimensional subbands by imposing a periodic boundary condition around the circumference. If the roll-up vector is <strong>C</strong>, single-valuedness requires
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="nanotube circumferential quantization condition">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.6em"/><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf C=2\pi q,\quad q\in\mathbb Z</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the graphene Brillouin zone this condition selects parallel lines of allowed wavevectors. A tube is metallic in the simplest zone-folding picture when one of those lines passes through a graphene Dirac point; otherwise the nearest allowed line misses the touching point and a gap appears. Chirality therefore converts a geometric boundary condition directly into an electronic distinction.
  </p>
  <p>
    Curvature shows the limits of pure zone folding. It changes bond equivalence and mixes π and σ character, shifting the crossing and opening small gaps in tubes that the flat-sheet model would classify as metallic. In sufficiently small tubes, stronger rehybridization can reorder bands qualitatively. Tight binding remains useful precisely because extra orbitals and curvature-dependent matrix elements can be added while retaining a transparent geometric interpretation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Boron nitride nanotubes illustrate the role of on-site asymmetry: replacing equivalent carbon sublattices by chemically distinct B and N sites introduces a large diagonal energy difference, so a robust gap remains even though the underlying rolled honeycomb geometry is similar.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-9-cuo2">
  <h2 id="section-14-9-cuo2">14.9 Square Lattice and CuO₂ Planes</h2>
  <p>
    The square-lattice one-band model acquires a deeper meaning in cuprate materials. A microscopic description near the Fermi level contains Cu d and O p states, and a three-orbital model naturally produces bonding, nonbonding, and antibonding combinations. The antibonding band that crosses the Fermi level can then be represented by an effective orbital centered on Cu with d<sub>x²−y²</sub> symmetry but substantial weight on the neighboring oxygen sites.
  </p>
  <p>
    Downfolding therefore changes what the “orbital” means. The effective one-band state is not a bare atomic Cu d orbital; it is an extended combination chosen to reproduce the low-energy subspace. Because its nearest-neighbor matrix elements have the same sign pattern on the square lattice, its dispersion can be mapped onto the algebra of a one-orbital square-lattice model. Longer-range hopping is nevertheless essential for quantitative Fermi-surface shape.
  </p>
  <p>
    This example is a useful template for model construction: a simple lattice Hamiltonian may faithfully represent a narrow energy window while its basis functions encode substantial chemistry from degrees of freedom that have been eliminated. Model simplicity should therefore be judged together with the subspace and observable it is designed to reproduce.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-10-materials">
  <h2 id="section-14-10-materials">14.10 Semiconductors and Transition Metals</h2>
  <p>
    The semiconductor and transition-metal examples show two very different uses of tight binding. In semiconductors, an extended s–p–d–s* parameterization can be fitted closely to experimental or higher-level band energies and then used efficiently in large device-scale geometries. Minimal bases can reproduce valence-band chemistry yet fail badly for conduction bands because the missing high-energy orbital character changes dispersion even when those states are not themselves occupied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Transition metals present the complementary situation: localized d orbitals naturally generate narrow d bands while more extended s character produces broad dispersive bands. Even approximate parameter tables can therefore expose the main qualitative band hierarchy across a whole material class. Quantitative predictions, however, still depend on the fitted energy window, magnetic state, structure, and parameterization.
  </p>
  <p>
    These cases reinforce a central theme of the chapter. Tight binding is not one accuracy level. It can be a pedagogical model with a handful of parameters, an interpolation device fitted to first-principles bands, or a structural model fitted simultaneously to energies and forces. The scientific claim must match the information used to construct the Hamiltonian.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-11-total-energy">
  <h2 id="section-14-11-total-energy">14.11 Total Energy, Force, and Stress in Tight-Binding</h2>
  <p>
    Band eigenvalues alone do not define a unique total energy. In self-consistent Kohn–Sham theory the eigenvalue sum must be corrected for double counting and nuclear interactions. An empirical tight-binding Hamiltonian does not explicitly carry the density functional needed to compute those corrections, so a structural model adds a separate short-ranged term fitted consistently with the electronic matrix elements.
  </p>
  <p>
    A common form writes the electronic contribution through the one-particle density matrix and adds a repulsive or correction energy depending on interatomic separations,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="tight binding total energy with pair correction">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>E</mi><mi>tot</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>Tr</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>+</mo>
          <munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;tot&#125;=\mathrm&#123;Tr&#125;(\rho H)+\sum_&#123;I&lt;J&#125;f(R_&#123;IJ&#125;)</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The split between the eigenvalue term and the correction is not unique: a geometry-dependent shift can be moved between them without changing the fitted total energy if the parameterization is changed consistently. Consequently, two tight-binding models with similar bands may have very different structural energetics.
  </p>
  <p>
    Once the total-energy expression is variational with respect to the occupied electronic state, forces follow by differentiating the geometry-dependent Hamiltonian and correction term,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="tight binding force expression">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi mathvariant="bold">F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Tr</mi>
          <mfenced><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mfenced>
          <mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></munder>
          <mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>J</mi></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf F_I=-\mathrm&#123;Tr&#125;\left(\rho\frac&#123;\partial H&#125;&#123;\partial\mathbf R_I&#125;\right)-\sum_&#123;J\ne I&#125;\frac&#123;\partial f(R_&#123;IJ&#125;)&#125;&#123;\partial\mathbf R_I&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Stress follows from the corresponding strain derivative. For nonorthogonal or explicitly environment-dependent models, the full derivative also includes the dependence of overlap and model parameters on geometry. A force formula is therefore only as consistent as the total-energy model from which it is differentiated.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-14-12-transferability">
  <h2 id="section-14-12-transferability">14.12 Transferability: Nonorthogonality and Environment Dependence</h2>
  <p>
    Transferability is the central limitation that closes the chapter. A two-center fit absorbs neglected multicenter physics into effective parameters. When coordination changes—from an open structure to a close-packed environment, for example—the omitted multicenter contribution changes, so a parameter that was accurate in the training structure need not remain accurate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two remedies recur. The first makes on-site and hopping terms explicitly environment dependent, so local coordination modifies the effective Hamiltonian. The second uses nonorthogonal atomic-like functions, which can remain compact and closer to transferable atomic shapes. The price is a generalized eigenvalue problem and the need to parameterize both Hamiltonian and overlap consistently.
  </p>
  <p>
    A representative environment-dependent on-site form uses a scalar neighborhood measure to shift each orbital level,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="environment dependent tight binding onsite energy">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo>
          <munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></munderover>
          <msubsup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></msubsup>
          <msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></msubsup><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_&#123;Il\sigma&#125;=\sum_&#123;n=0&#125;^3 b_&#123;Il\sigma&#125;^&#123;(n)&#125;\rho_&#123;I\sigma&#125;^&#123;2n/3&#125;</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Such flexibility can produce excellent agreement across multiple structures when the fit includes those environments, but parameter count is not a substitute for validation outside the fitted domain. Transferability must be tested on structures, magnetic states, volumes, and observables that were not merely used to determine the parameters.
  </p>
  <p>
    The chapter therefore ends at exactly the boundary needed for Chapter 15. Localized orbitals themselves are not the approximation. The approximation in empirical tight binding is replacing explicit basis functions and self-consistent matrix-element evaluation by a parameterized Hamiltonian and overlap model. Full localized-orbital electronic-structure methods retain the same matrix algebra while calculating those ingredients from the underlying electronic problem.
  </p>
</section>
